Hi people,

My previous two ascensions were a Wiz and a Tou, so after some period of
real Life I decided to try a non-spellcasting character. Cavemen seemed
to be the most despised fighter characters, so I wanted to try out
whether they really suck as much as the rumour says. I also wanted a
lawful for a change.

My first two promising cavemen died incredibly stupid deaths due to
typos and trying to descend without necessary equipment and getting,
e.g., stranded on Medusa island with ALL equipment on the other side
(how on earth can you climb out of a pool of water into a nearby
*hole*?). I tried to make them wishless for a change, which resulted in
polytrap problems.

This time I got an early fountain wish on DL1 which I spent on GDSM (got
blessed +0), partly to protect from polytraps. I also hit Ranger bones
in the Mines, and got a (cursed) +2 CoDisp and +0 GoP from there (other
stuff, including the +2 arrows and a CoMR which I found by chance,
turned out to be useless in the end). The bones' monsters were hard even
with such armor, so I turned back and did Sokoban for an AoRefl. There
were also Arc bones in the mines, which I left untouched except for the
sack. The Minetown altar was coaligned. Later I got some tolerable pets,
but lost them. No more interesting stuff until much later in the game.
The first altar an Astral I visited was lawful (Famine; /oDeath, an
accident).

Differences from previous games include:

 * only two wishes used (fountain GDSM + =oLev before the Planes)
 * no large sacfests (i.e. no long, tedious sac/sac/sac/pray sequences)
 * not using spells (obviously)
 * no speed/jumping boots or magic headgear found
 * deliberately not using ranged *weapons* (wands are OK)
 * absolutely no leviation till Air (didn't find any means...)
 * no regeneration
 * meleeing `;' wearing an oilskin cloak
 * getting fire resistance only deep in Hell (no matter how much I ate)
 * getting see invisible only deep in Hell (after killing most demons)
 * wielding the dipped-for Excalibur for drain protection (CoDisp=MC2)
 * finding Grayswandir late deep in Hell and stashing it into the BoH
 * finding a magic lamp only deep in Hell
 * abundancy of magic markers (never even had to recharge one!)
 * more use of C?oTele to navigate through Gehennom
 * using Asmodeus to map part of Hell from the VS level upwards
 * paying for clairvoyance to find Vlad
 * Vlad commited suicide with his wand of magic missile
 * someone summoned Juiblex (thus no ``Juiblex express'')
 * recharging Orcus' wand for Rodney et al. a couple of times.
 * using teleport, not polymorph, to get rid of air elementals on Astral
 * not getting intrinsic teleportitis or teleport control
 * finding the VS only after killing Rodney
 * almost never going Burdened (better item management)
 * using a cursed bag of holding to get rid of trolls (takes no turns)
 * carrying the Sceptre for conflict (which turned out to be cool)
 * somewhat faster than my previous games (which were ~70k turns)

The synopsis is: Cavemen start with an excellent collection of stones
which not only serve as a starting handicap for packrats, but can be
sold in shops for a terrific price (they're definitely worth it). A
crystal ball helps in Hell with non-fireproof armor and no fire
resistance, and also in the Planes. MC2 ascension with ordinary
boots/shield/helm is certainly doable. As the dragons' AI makes them not
use their breath attack when nearby, meleeing the Chromatic Dragon might
be a good idea if you have MR. Minotaurs are machines for turning HP
into /oDigging charges. Asmodeus with a wand of digging can map Hell
both ways, which can be annoying. Dispater should be dispatched at first
sight as he has the habit of summoning nasties. Being lawful means you
spend more time in Hell and meet more interesting creatures; conflict
helps, the Sceptre rocks.

